1 NAME Charlotte /d'Halluin/ Dame d’Halluin; Dame de Canaples (by marriage)
2 GIVN Charlotte
2 SURN d'Halluin
2 NSFX Dame d’Halluin; Dame de Canaples (by marriage)
2 _MARNM Beaufort
1 NAME /d’Halluin/
2 GIVN
2 SURN d’Halluin
1 NAME /van Halewijn/
2 GIVN
2 SURN van Halewijn
1 SEX F
1 OCCU Dame d’Halluin; Dame de Canaples (by marriage); Vicomtesse de Furnes (by maternal right).
1 TITL (Gen 15)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} While her brothers were managing the family’s Flemish heartland and French court positions, Charlotte’s marriage into the House of Beaufort secured the family’s influence in the Somme valley. This was a vital strat
2 CONC egic region that acted as a buffer between the French crown and the Burgundian/Habsburg territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charlotte d’Halluin (de Beaufort), Grande Dame; Dame d’Halluin; Dame de Canaples (by marriage); Vicomtesse de Furnes (by maternal right).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte was the 'Senior Envoy' of the Comines line; as the daughter of the Governor of Lille and the Viscountess of Furnes, her marriage into the House of Beaufort-Canaples exported the immense legal and territorial weight of th
2 CONC e Flemish high-peerage into the heart of the French military aristocracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Narrative Bio'''
2 CONT Charlotte d’Halluin was raised at the absolute intersection of power and law. As the daughter of Philippe, she grew up in the shadow of the Château de Comines and the administrative halls of Lille. Her mother, Françoise de Ghistel
2 CONC les, was one of the most significant heiresses of her generation, holding the Vicomté de Furnes—a title that carried immense jurisdictional weight in West Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Charlotte married Jean de Beaufort, Seigneur de Canaples, it was a merger of the "Sword" (Beaufort) and the "Statute" (Halluin/Ghistelles). In the Archives of the Somme, Charlotte appears not merely as a wife, but as a primar
2 CONC y agent in the management of the Canaples and Heuchin territories. She was the matriarch who ensured that the "Comines" influence remained a factor in French politics, providing a safe haven for her Flemish kin during the frequent
2 CONC  wars between Francis I and Charles V.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The "Ghistelles-Halluin" Legacy'''
2 CONT The most significant aspect of Charlotte’s identity was her connection to the House of Ghistelles. Through her mother, she was linked to the ancient "Sovereign" traditions of Flanders. This made her marriage to Jean de Beaufort a 
2 CONC massive social upgrade for the Beauforts, as they were now kin to the Vicomtes of Furnes, giving them a legitimate claim to interfere in Flemish border politics.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT The Beaufort-Ghistelles-Halluin Shield: A complex and high-status visual brand.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blazon: Argent, three lions rampant gules (red) [Beaufort], impaling a quarterly shield: 1 & 4 Sable, a cross argent [Halluin]; 2 & 3 Gules, a chevron ermine [Ghistelles].
2 CONT
2 CONT Visual Note: In your research, the presence of the Ermine Chevron of Ghistelles on her personal seals is the "smoking gun." It proves her descent from the Viscountess of Furnes and distinguishes her from the "Piennes" cousins.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Primary Sources (The "Agatha" Trail)'''
2 CONT * Archives de l'État (Bruges) - Fonds Ghistelles / Seigneurie de Dudzele: Marriage contracts (c. 1520) detailing the dowry provided by Philippe d'Halluin and the specific lordships (Merckem, Dudzele) inherited through the Ghistell
2 CONC es line.
2 CONT * Archives Départementales du Nord (Lille) - Chambre des Comptes: Records of the "Relief" (feudal succession tax) paid for Charlotte's Flemish holdings, explicitly naming her as the daughter of Philippe d'Halluin and Françoise de 
2 CONC Ghistelles.
2 CONT * The "Furnes" Registry (Vieur-Vix-Vorme): Documents citing Charlotte as the legal successor to her mother's rights in the Vicomté de Furnes, utilized during border disputes.
2 CONT * Généalogie de la Maison de Beaufort (Du Chesne, 1619): Correctly identifies her parentage and her role in bringing the Flemish "Senior" titles into the Canaples lineage.
2 CONT * Seals of the Bailliage d'Amiens: Physical wax seals from 1532 showing the impaled Halluin/Ghistelles arms, confirming her visual identity and rank.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Genealogical Data Points'''
2 CONT Father: (Gen 14) Philippe d'Halluin, Seigneur de Comines, Governor of Lille.
2 CONT Mother: (Gen 14) Françoise de Ghistelles, Dame de Dudzele, Vicomtesse de Furnes.
2 CONT Spouse: (Gen 15) Jean de Beaufort, Seigneur de Canaples.
2 CONT Key Heir: Louis de Beaufort-Canaples, who inherited the combined prestige of the Beaufort, Halluin, and Ghistelles lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Summary Notes:'''
2 CONT 1. Charlotte was the "Senior Envoy to Picardy." She was the reason the Beauforts became an extension of the Comines power base. She provided the "High-Peerage" blood of the Ghistelles and the administrative weight of the Halluins 
2 CONC to a frontier family that was already a military powerhouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT
