1 TITL (Gen 15)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Marguerite d'Halluin was the bridge between regional Flemish lordship and the supreme administrative and Imperial power of the era.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marguerite d’Halluin (de Glymes), Grande Dame; Dame d’Halluin; Dame de Walhain (by marriage); Co-Heiress of the Vicomté de Furnes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite was the 'Imperial Court Pivot' for the House of Comines; as the daughter of the Governor of Lille, her marriage into the House of Glymes-Berghes placed the senior Halluin influence at the very heart of the Habsburg admi
2 CONC nistration in Brussels.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Narrative Bio'''
2 CONT Marguerite d’Halluin was raised in the administrative epicenter of Flanders—the court of her father, Philippe, in Lille. Her marriage was a masterstroke of "Centralized Loyalty." While the "Piennes" branch of the family often lean
2 CONC ed toward French interests, Marguerite's life was centered in the Duchy of Brabant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Archives of the Council of Brabant, Marguerite is recorded as a formidable manager of the Seigneurie of Walhain. She brought the refined, scholarly culture of the Comines household to the Glymes family, acting as a bridge b
2 CONC etween the border-warrior traditions of her father and the sophisticated court life of Brussels. She was a vital point of contact for the Halluin family's legal affairs, often intervening in the Great Council of Mechelen to protec
2 CONC t the jurisdictional rights of the Vicomté de Furnes and the Dudzele estates she co-inherited with her sister Charlotte.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The "Ghistelles-Walhain" Inheritance'''
2 CONT The most critical aspect of Marguerite’s identity was her status as a full daughter of Françoise de Ghistelles. Together with her sister Charlotte, she represented the Ghistelles maternal block. While her cousin Georges (son of Je
2 CONC an IV) held the Halluin name and the Comines seat, Marguerite and Charlotte held the Ghistelles bloodline, which carried specific territorial rights in Dudzele, Merckem, and Furnes that were distinct from the main Halluin patrimon
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT The Glymes-Berghes Arms:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blazon: Sable, a lion rampant Or, armed and langued gules; a border engrailed gules (for the Walhain branch) [Glymes], impaling a quarterly shield: 1 & 4 Sable, a cross argent [Halluin]; 2 & 3 Gules, a chevron ermine [Ghistelles].
2 CONT
2 CONT Visual Note: The presence of the Ermine Chevron of Ghistelles on her personal seals is the "Agatha" proof of her parentage, distinguishing her from any children of her father's other relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Primary Sources (The "Agatha" Trail)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Archives de l'État (Brussels) - Fonds de la Maison de Glymes: Detailed records of the marriage settlement (c. 1525) and the joint administration of the Walhain estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Archives de l'État (Bruges) - Fonds Ghistelles: Succession documents citing Marguerite and Charlotte as the primary heiresses to the Ghistelles-Dudzele estates, separate from the Halluin-Comines main line.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The "Généalogie des Berghes" (Butkens, 1626): A foundational printed source that confirms the marriage to the daughter of Philippe d'Halluin and Françoise de Ghistelles.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Inscriptions Funéraires du Brabant: Monumental evidence from the church at Walhain, identifying her by her full titles and her maternal descent from the House of Ghistelles.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Genealogical Data Points'''
2 CONT Father: (Gen 14) Philippe d'Halluin, Seigneur de Comines.
2 CONT Mother: (Gen 14) Françoise de Ghistelles, Dame de Dudzele, Vicomtesse de Furnes.
2 CONT Spouse: (Gen 15) Antoine de Glymes, Seigneur de Walhain.
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT Charlotte d'Halluin (Full sister, m. Jean de Beauffort).
2 CONT Georges d'Halluin (First Cousin, the Humanist).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Research Notes:'''
2 CONT 1. Marguerite as the "Imperial Court Pivot." She is the reason the senior line maintained its status in the capital. She provided the Legal and Social Gravity that kept the Halluin-Ghistelles name at the center of the Habsburg pow
2 CONC er structure.
2 CONT
2 CONT
