2 CONT
2 CONT France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	8 September 1669
2 CONT
2 CONT Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Capucins, Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise de Lorraine (November 1592 – 8 September 1669) was a French noble women and the daughter in law of King Henry IV of France; sometimes known as Françoise de Mercœur, she was the niece of Louise of Lorraine, wife of the pr
2 CONC evious king, Henry III of France. Through her daughter, Françoise was a great great great grandfather of Louis XV of France, and thus an ancestor of Prince Henri the Count of Paris, Orléanist pretender to the French throne, are am
2 CONC ong the male line descendants and heirs of Philippe. He is also an ancestor of Juan Carlos I of Spain, of Albert II, King of the Belgians, of Henri, Grand Duke of Luxembourg, and of Vittorio Emanuele, Prince of Naples, a pretender
2 CONC  to the Italian throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 References and notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise was the youngest of two children she was born in November 1592 with the exact date unknown; her only sibling born Philippe Louis de Lorraine died in 1590 aged one year old; as such from her birth to the Duke and Duchess 
2 CONC of Mercœur, she was an heiress to one of the largest private fortunes of her age. Her marriage was sealed when she was just a child thanks to her father;
2 CONT
2 CONT During the reign of Henry III, her father had put himself at the head of the League in Brittany, and had himself proclaimed protector of the Roman Catholic Church in the province in 1588. Invoking the hereditary rights of his wife
2 CONC , who was a descendant of the Dukes of Brittany, he endeavoured to make himself independent in that province, and organized a government at Nantes, calling his son "prince and duke of Brittany".
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth, her only daughter, c.1652
2 CONT
2 CONT With the aid of the Spaniards he defeated the Duke of Montpensier, whom Henry IV of France had sent against him, at Craon in 1592. The king marched against Mercœur in person, and received his submission at Angers on 20 March 1598.
2 CONT
2 CONT The French and Spanish then signed the Peace of Vervins on 2 May 1598 when Françoise was 6; part of the treaty was that the young Françoise would be engaged to the illegitimate child of Henry IV, the Duke of Vendôme and the Mercœu
2 CONC r fortune would then be absorbed into the House of Bourbon . Vendôme was the eldest child of the King and his mistress Gabrielle d'Estrées. As he was illegitimate, he was unable to inherit the throne.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage ceremony took place at the Palace of Fontainebleau on 16 July 1609. The bride was 17; the groom was just 14. The couple had three children overall and were the paternal grand parents of the famous débauché general, le
2 CONC  Grand Vendôme.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Françoise was the to the Duchess of Mercœur and Penthièvre since 1602 at the time of her fathers death, as part of the marriage contract these lands automatically revert to César.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her husband died in 1665 a disgraced man having been involved in the Fronde and been accused of trying to poison the all powerful Cardinal Richelieu. In the same year she went to Savoy with her grand daughter Marie Jeanne of Savoy
2 CONC  for the wedding of of Marie Jeanne to Charles Emmanuel II, Duke of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The duchies of Mercœur and Penthièvre were thus inherited by her eldest son who married Laura Mancini in 1651, niece of Cardinal Mazarin[2]. Françoise died in Paris in 1669 aged 77. She was buried in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other fiefdoms that Françoise owned were the Principality of Martigues, Duchy of Étampes and Lordship of Ancenis.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme (1612–1669) married Laura Mancini and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elisabeth de Bourbon, Mademoiselle de Vendôme (Paris, August 1614 – Paris, 19 May 1664), married in Paris on 11 July 1643 Charles Amadeus of Savoy, Duke of Nemours and has issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * François de Bourbon, Duke of Beaufort (1616–1669) never married;
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References and notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Henri IV had had no children with his first wife Margaret of Valois; when in 1600 Henry married Marie de' Medici, she gave birth to six children in quick succession including the future Louis XIII whom César was rasied
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ César agreed to this due to his part in the Fronde of the nobles
2 CONT
