2 CONT
2 CONT Philip of Spain, Duke of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla (15 March 1720 – 18 July 1765) was duke of Parma from 1748 to 1765. He founded the House of Bourbon-Parma (a.k.a the Bourbons of Parma), a cadet line of the Spanish branch of 
2 CONC the dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.1 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Titles and Styles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Born at the Royal Alcazar in Madrid, he was the fourth child and third son of Philip V of Spain and his wife, Elisabeth of Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was raised in Madrid and as a child showed more interest in art than in politics. He was also the 12th Count of Chinchón Grandee of Spain First Class with a Coat of Arms of de Bourbon after the alienation with Royal Authorizati
2 CONC on in 1738 of the 11th Conde Don Jose Sforza-Cesarini, Duke of Canzano, title he later ceded to his brother Louis in 1754.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother came from the family of Farnese, which had ruled the Duchy of Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla for many generations. The Duchy had been ruled between 1731 and 1736 by his elder brother Charles, but exchanged with Austria f
2 CONC or The Two Sicilies after the War of Polish Succession. Twelve years later, in the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748), Austria lost the Duchy, and Philip became the new Duke, founding the House of Bourbon-Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of the Treaty of Versailles (1757) between Austria and France it was intended that Phillip would become King of the Southern Netherlands, in a deal that would see French troops occupy key positions in the country - however
2 CONC  this arrangement was repudiated by the subsequent Third Treaty of Versailles and Phillip continued in Parma.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Duchy of Parma was ruined by many years of warfare, and in 1759 Philip named the abled Frenchmen Guillaume du Tillot as his minister to restore the economy. Philip was an enlightened ruler. He stimulated education and philosop
2 CONC hy, attracting personalities like Étienne Bonnot de Condillac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip, by Louis René Vialy, engraved by Jean-Joseph Balechou (1740)
2 CONT
2 CONT
