1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Links:==
2 CONT *[http://thepeerage.com/p11073.htm#i110727 The Peerage]
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/I/per_page.php?id=5436 Geneall:]
2 CONT *[http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Amalia_of_the_Two_Sicilies Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Amalia Teresa of the Two Sicilies (26 April 1782 – 24 March 1866) was Queen of the French from 1830-1848, consort to King Louis-Philippe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not to be confused with Princess Maria Amalia of Bourbon-Two Sicilies (1818-1857).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT Her confessor: https://www.geni.com/people/Domenico-Gaetano-Cavalcanti/6000000174347465903
2 CONT
2 CONT “The beautiful set of sapphires were just three of the pieces taken on Sunday in the Louvre jewel theft. Here is a little more of the history behind the jewels. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie-Amélie de Bourbon-Siciles was born on April 26, 1782, in the Kingdom of Naples. She was one of the 18 children of King Ferdinand of Naples and Queen Marie-Caroline of Austria, sister of Marie Antoinette. On November 28, 1809
2 CONC , she married Louis-Philippe, duc d’Orleans and King of France from 1830-1848. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1821, Louis-Philippe purchased a set of sapphire jewelry from Hortense, daughter of Josephine de Beauharnais and Queen of Holland. Napoleon adopted Hortense and her brother and raised them as his own and gave her hand in marria
2 CONC ge to his brother Louis Napoleon, she wasn’t so thrilled with this idea. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hortense acquired many jewels through all branches of her family, which stayed in the family until purchased by Louis-Philippe for 160,000 francs. A gift to his wife, Queen Marie-Amélie, who had the settings changed, including red
2 CONC ucing the size of the tiara. The jewels spent the next century in the Orleans family until 1985, when they were sold to the Louvre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The French crown jewel collection has changed many times since in four hundred years. In 1525, François Ier began to add to it before heading to be imprisoned in Spain. During the Revolution in 1792, much of the collection was sto
2 CONC len, including the Hope diamond, and luckily, all were recovered. On May 12, 1887, an auction was held selling off thousands of gems to fund the country. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, in the Galerie d’Apollon, the jewels on display date to Napoleon and later include the stunning sapphire pieces of Marie-Amélie. Forty-four sapphires surrounded by more than 2,500 diamonds in six pieces glisten in the light
2 CONC . The Ceylon sapphires were not heated, so the blue is a tad lighter than the deep sapphires you see in current jewelry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT That is until Sunday, October 19, 2025, the thieves took the tiara, necklace and earrings, but lef the three brooches behind and another set of earrings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1836, artist Louis Hersent painted the queen wearing many pieces, including a tiara far larger than the one on display by her descendants.” Bleu, Blonde and Rouge
