1 TITL Lt. Gen. Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} During the British colonial rule of the Indian Subcontinent, the ancient Hindu tradition of Sati was still practiced: The burning of widows alive, on the funeral pyres of their dead husbands, by force if necessary.
2 CONT
2 CONT This triggered maybe the most British threat ever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Depiction of the Sati custom; as the flames consume the woman, she is surrounded by men prepared to force her back into the flames.
2 CONT
2 CONT The British — horrified by these barbaric practices — passed laws, making Sati and the equally foul tradition of stoning lepers to death, illegal. This enraged Hindu Priests, who gathered a large mob to protest the interference in
2 CONC  their religion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enter Sir Charles Napier:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Be it so. This burning of widows is your custom; prepare the funeral pile. But my nation has also a custom. When men burn women alive we hang them, and confiscate all their property. My carpenters shall therefore erect gibbets on
2 CONC  which to hang all concerned when the widow is consumed. Let us all act according to national customs.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Charles J Napier, Commander of British forces in India, 1843–1847.
2 CONT
2 CONT The mob dispersed, the priests quickly vanished, and the widows were no longer burned alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Quora Added 12/12/20 by Janet Milburn (8th cousin 6 times removed)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_James_Napier
2 CONT
2 CONT General Sir Charles James Napier, GCB (10 August 1782 – 29 August 1853), was a general of the British Empire and the British Army's Commander-in-Chief in India, notable for conquering the Sindh Province in what is now Pakistan.
2 CONT
2 CONT His genealogy
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Colonel (the Honourable) George Napier and his second wife, Lady Sarah Lennox, with this being the second marriage for both parties. Lady Sarah was the great-granddaughter of King Charles II. Napier was bo
2 CONC rn at the Whitehall Palace in London, and he received part of his education at boarding school in Celbridge, Ireland. Napier joined the 33rd Infantry Regiment of the British Army in 1794, and decided to become a career soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Peninsular War
2 CONT
2 CONT Napier commanded the 50th (Queen's Own) Regiment of Foot during Peninsular War in Iberia against Napoleon Bonaparte. Napier's activities there ended during the Battle of Corunna, in which he was wounded and left for dead on the ba
2 CONC ttlefield. Napier was rescued, barely alive, by a French Army drummer named Guibert, and taken as a prisoner-of-war. Nevertheless, Napier was awarded an Army Gold Medal after he was returned to British hands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napier recuperated from his wounds while he was being held near the headquarters of the French Marshall Soult, and then somehow he was returned to the British Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napier volunteered to return to the Iberian Peninsula in 1810 to fight again against Napoleon in Portugal - notably in the Battle of the Côa, where he had two horses shot out from under him, in the Battle of Bussaco, in the Battle
2 CONC  of Fuentes de Onoro, and in the Battle of Badajoz (1812) (the second siege of Badajoz) in Castile, Spain, in which he was a lieutenant colonel in the 102nd regiment. For his deeds at Bussaco and at Fuentes de Oñoro, Napier won th
2 CONC e silver medal with two clasps.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1838, Napier returned to England to become the General Officer Commanding of the British Northern District.
2 CONT
2 CONT Service in India
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1842, at the age of 60, Napier was appointed Major General to the command of the Indian army within the Bombay Presidency. Here Lord Ellenborough's policy led Napier to Sindh Province (Scinde), for the purpose of quelling the i
2 CONC nsurrection of the Muslim rulers who had remained hostile to the British Empire following the First Anglo-Afghan War. Napier's campaign against these chieftains resulted in victories in the Battle of Miani (Meanee) against General
2 CONC  Hoshu Sheedi and the Battle of Hyderabad, and then the subjugation of the Sindh Province, and its annexation by its eastern neighbors.
2 CONT
2 CONT His orders had been only to put down the rebels, and by conquering the whole Sindh Province he greatly exceeded his mandate. Napier was supposed to have despatched to his superiors the short, notable message, "Peccavi", the Latin 
2 CONC for "I have sinned" (which was a pun on I have Sindh). This pun appeared in a cartoon in Punch magazine in 1844 beneath a caricature of Charles Napier. The true author of the pun was, however, Catherine Winkworth, an English girl 
2 CONC then in her teens, who submitted it to Punch, which then printed it as a factual report. Later proponents of British rule over the East Indians justified the conquest thus: "If this was a piece of rascality, it was a noble piece o
2 CONC f rascality!"
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 July 1843, Napier was appointed Knight Grand Cross in the military division of the Order of the Bath, in recognition of his leading the victories at Miani and Hyderabad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Napier was appointed Governor of the Bombay Presidency by Lord Ellenborough. However, under his leadership the administration clashed with the policies of the directors of the British East India Company, and Napier was accordingly
2 CONC  removed from office and returned home in disgust. Napier was again dispatched to India during the spring of 1849, in order to obtain the submission of the Sikhs. However upon arriving once again in India, Napier found that this h
2 CONC ad already been accomplished by Lord Gough and his army.
2 CONT
2 CONT A story for which Napier is often noted involved Hindu priests complaining to him about the prohibition of Sati by British authorities. This was the custom of burning a widow alive on the funeral pyre of her husband. As first reco
2 CONC unted by his brother William, he replied:
2 CONT
2 CONT  "Be it so. This burning of widows is your custom; prepare the funeral pile. But my nation has also a custom. When men burn women alive we hang them, and confiscate all their property. My carpenters shall therefore erect gibbets o
2 CONC n which to hang all concerned when the widow is consumed. Let us all act according to national customs." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Napier remained for a while as the Commander-in-Chief in India. He also quarrelled repeatedlly with Lord Dalhousie, the Governor-General of India. Finally, Napier resigned from his post in India, and returned home to England for t
2 CONC he last time. Napier was still suffering with physical infirmities which were results of his wounds during the Peninsular War, and he died about two years later at Oaklands, near Portsmouth, England, on 29 August 1853, at the age 
2 CONC of 71. Napier's former house is now part of Oaklands Catholic School of Waterlooville. His remains were buried in the Royal Garrison Church in Portsmouth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Views on subduing insurgencies
2 CONT
2 CONT General Napier put down several insurgencies in India during his reign as Commander-in-Chief in India, and once said of his philosophy about how to do so effectively:
2 CONT
2 CONT  The best way to quiet a country is a good thrashing, followed by great kindness afterwards. Even the wildest chaps are thus tamed.
2 CONT
2 CONT which may help explain why he felt rebellions should be suppressed with such brutality.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also once said that:
2 CONT
2 CONT "the human mind is never better disposed to gratitude and attachment than when softened by fear."
2 CONT
2 CONT An implementation of this theory would be after the Battle of Miani, where most of the Mirs surrendered. One leader held back and was told by Napier:
2 CONT
2 CONT Come here instantly. Come here at once and make your submission, or I will in a week tear you from the midst of your village and hang you.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also mused that:
2 CONT
2 CONT "so perverse is mankind that every nationality prefers to be misgoverned by its own people than to be well ruled by another"
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorials
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1903, the 25th Bombay Rifles (which as the 25th Regiment of Bombay Native Infantry had formed part of Napier's force in the conquest of Sindh) was renamed the 125th Napier's Rifles. Since amalgamated, it is now the 5th Battalio
2 CONC n (Napier's) of the Rajputana Rifles.
2 CONT
2 CONT A statue in honour of Sir Charles Napier by George Gamon Adams (1821–1898) is on the southwest plinth, of the four plinths in Trafalgar Square, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT The city of Napier in the Hawke's Bay region of New Zealand is named after Sir Charles Napier. The suburb of Meeanee commemorates his victory in the Battle of Miani.
2 CONT
2 CONT The city of Karachi in Sindh (Pakistan) earlier had a Napier Road (now Shahrah-e-Altaf Hussain), Napier Street (now Mir Karamali Talpur Road) and Napier Barracks (now Liaquat Barracks) on Shara-e-Faisal. In the port area, there is
2 CONC  also a Napier Mole. In Manora, the St. Paul's Church, erected in 1864, is a memorial to Napier.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Napier Gardens in Argostoli on the Greek island of Kefalonia are named after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some ten pubs in England are named after him, either as the Sir Charles Napier, or the General Napier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Karachi Grammar School named its second-oldest house "Napier" after Sir Charles Napier (the oldest House is named Frere after Sir Henry Bartle Frere).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT There are numerous references to Napier on the Internet.  
2 CONT  Probably the best for Napier lineage is this - http://auden.stanford.edu/cgi-bin/auden/individual.php?pid=I18503&ged=auden-bicknell.ged&tab=0
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is another:  www.historyhome.co.uk/peel/people/napier.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT His statue is located 100 metres from the front entrance of the Athenaeum, Pall Mall, London.  To the left of the staircase down to St. James' Park, which Napier surveys.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The 'peccavi' (I have Sindh) general
