1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Information source: http://www.florin.ms/epitaphs.html
2 CONT
2 CONT His chief claim to notice is that he was the author of ‘Florentine History from the earliest Authentic Records to the Accession of Ferdinand the Third, Grandduke of Tuscany,’ six vols., 1846-7, a work showing much independence of 
2 CONC judgment and vivacity of style, but marred by prolixity. He was elected a fellow of the Royal Society on 18 May 1820, and died at 62 Cadogan Place, London, on 13 Oct. 1853. He married on 17 Nov. 1823 Caroline Bennet, a natural dau
2 CONC ghter of Charles Lennox, third duke of Richmond; she died at Florence on 5 Sept. 1836, leaving three children'
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Edward_Napier
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Edward Napier (1789–1853) was a British naval officer and historian.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Napier, born on 5 March 1789, was son of Colonel George Napier, younger brother of Sir Charles James Napier, conqueror of Scinde, of Sir George Thomas Napier, governor of the Cape of Good Hope, and of Sir William Francis Patrick N
2 CONC apier, historian and general.
2 CONT
2 CONT He entered the Royal Naval Academy on 5 May 1803, and, embarking on 20 September 1806 on board the HMS Spencer, 74 guns, was present in the expedition against Copenhagen in 1807, and assisted at the destruction of Fleckeröe Castle
2 CONC  on the coast of Norway. From 1808 to 1811 he served in the East Indies, and on 4 May 1810 received his commission as lieutenant. On 7 June 1814 he was promoted to the command of the Goree, 18 guns, and, soon after removing to the
2 CONC  Rifleman, 18 guns, was for a considerable time entrusted with the charge of the trade in the Bay of Fundy. In August 1815 he went on half-pay, having previously declined a piece of plate which had been voted to him for his care i
2 CONC n the conduct of convoys between the port of St. John's, New Brunswick, and Castine. On 31 December 1830 he was gazetted to the rank of captain, and was put on half-pay.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was elected a fellow of the Royal Society on 18 May 1820, and died at 62 Cadogan Place, London, on 13 October 1853.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT His chief claim to notice is that he was the author of ‘Florentine History from the earliest Authentic Records to the Accession of Ferdinand the Third, Grandduke of Tuscany,’ six vols., 1846–7, a work showing much independence of 
2 CONC judgment and vivacity of style, but marred by prolixity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married on 17 November 1823 Caroline Bennet, a natural daughter of Charles Lennox, 3rd Duke of Richmond; she was his first cousin, since Napier's mother was Lady Sarah Lennox, sister of the Duke. Caroline died at Florence on 5 
2 CONC September 1836, leaving three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * '''Residence''': The Lawn, Hemel Hampstead - 1830
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Hemel Hempstead, Hertfordshire, England - June 7 1841
