1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_George_Stuart_(Royal_Navy_officer)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord George Stuart, CB (1 Mar 1780 – 19 February 1841 at Balls Park, Hertfordshire) was a Rear-Admiral of the Blue in the Royal Navy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT
2 CONT He was from a side-branch of the Stuart family, descended from the Scottish King Robert II. He was the seventh son of John Stuart, 1st Marquess of Bute and his wife Charlotte Jane. He received the courtesy title Lord Stuart after 
2 CONC his father became the first Marquess of Bute in 1796. Among his brothers were Charles Stuart, who died in 1796 aboard HMS Leda and Captain William Stuart RN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT After attending Eton College, he joined the Royal Navy in November 1793 as a midshipman. In 1795 he took part in the second voyage of the HMS Providence under Captain William Robert Broughton, during which they went in search of G
2 CONC eorge Vancouver to the Pacific coast of North America and later to Japan and Macau. After the wreck of HMS Providence on a coral reef at Miyako-jima, Stuart and 29 seamen finally returned to Great Britain on board an East Indiaman
2 CONC  in 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was promoted to lieutenant in 1800 and commander in 1802.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 March 1804 he was appointed captain and took command of 44-gun frigate HMS Sheerness in the Indian Ocean; he was not aboard HMS Sheerness when she sank in a heavy storm off Trincomalee on 7 January 1805. On 8 April 1806 as co
2 CONC mmander of the frigate HMS Duncan he captured the French privateer Île de France in the North Sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1807 he was appointed captain of the 32-gun frigate HMS l'Aimable, with which he captured another French privateer. In the summer of 1808 l'Aimable escorted Wellesley's army from Cork to Portugal. On 3 February 1809 he capture 
2 CONC of the French frigate Iris after a 28-hour pursuit in the North Sea. In July 1809 he assumed command of a light squadron at the mouth of the Elbe and on July 7, a landing party destroyed French coastal batteries nearby and occupie
2 CONC d the city for a short time. On July 29, the French occupied the town of Geestendorf, on the Weser 28 miles from Cuxhaven. Learning of this Captain Stuart decided to march cross-country and attack the town. He succeeded in this an
2 CONC d re-took it. A few days later Duke Friedrich-Wilhelm of Braunschweig-Oel and his army arrived on the opposite bank of the Weser and, because the fortress at Geestendorf had been destroyed was able to embark there and escape destr
2 CONC uction.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1810, Stuart became commander of the frigate HMS Horatio, he occupied the Dutch Zierikzee in December 1813 and expelled the French forces from the island of Schouwen.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the British-American War he commanded the new 58-gun ship HMS Newcastle (1813).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1815 he was appointed a Companion of the Order of the Bath and later served as Naval Aide-de-camp of King William IV. In 1837 he was promoted to Rear Admiral of the Blue.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Jane Stewart (died 1862), a daughter of Major General James Stewart, on 7 October 1800. The couple had several children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT Emily Frances Stuart (died 1886) who married Charles Abbott, son of Charles Abbott, 1st Baron Tenterden
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Jane Stuart (1803–1877) who married John Townshend, 4. Marquess Townshend
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Stuart (1808–1880) who married Cecilia Hammersley, daughter of Charles Hammersley
