1 NOTE {geni:about_me} William Berkeley, 1st Earl FitzHardinge From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia The Right Honourable
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl FitzHardinge Lord-Lieutenant of Gloucestershire In office 18 December 1835 – 10 October 1857 Monarch 	William IV Victoria Preceded by 	The Duke of Beaufort Succeeded by 	The Earl of Ducie Personal details Born 	26 Decembe
2 CONC r 1786 Mount Street, Grosvenor Square, London Died 	10 October 1857 Berkeley Castle, Gloucestershire Nationality 	British Spouse(s) 	Unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT William FitzHardinge Berkeley, 1st Earl FitzHardinge (26 December 1786 – 10 October 1857), known as The Lord Segrave between 1831 and 1841, was a British landowner and politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT     1 Background
2 CONT     2 Dispute over legitimacy
2 CONT     3 Claim to barony and earldom of Berkeley
2 CONT     4 Public appointments
2 CONT     5 Personal life
2 CONT     6 References
2 CONT     7 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Background
2 CONT
2 CONT Berkeley was born at Mount Street, Grosvenor Square, London, the eldest son of Frederick Berkeley, 5th Earl of Berkeley, by Mary Cole, daughter of William Cole. He was the brother of Maurice Berkeley, 1st Baron FitzHardinge, Henry
2 CONC  FitzHardinge Berkeley, Grantley Berkeley and Craven Berkeley and the nephew of Sir George Cranfield-Berkeley.[1] Dispute over legitimacy
2 CONT
2 CONT There was great uncertainty over the validity of his parents' marriage. On 16 May 1796 Lord Berkeley married Mary Cole at Lambeth.[2] The Earl alleged that a previous marriage between them had occurred at Berkeley, Gloucestershire
2 CONC , officiated by the Vicar of Berkeley, on 30 March 1785. This ceremony was, however, kept secret until after the Lambeth marriage, with Mary being known as Miss Tudor between the two dates. Shortly after the Lambeth marriage a cer
2 CONC tificate of the Berkeley ceremony was produced, having been recoved, it was alleged, under very strange circumstances. The couple had had six sons up to the marriage of 1796, including William Berkeley. In 1799, after the Earl had
2 CONC  announced his former marriage, William Berkeley (who was commonly styled Viscount Dursley, the normal courtesy title for the heir apparent to the earldom) obtained leave to prove his legitimacy before the Committee for Privileges
2 CONC  of the House of Lords, and in 1801 the Earl made a deposition giving full details of the Berkeley marriage. Claim to barony and earldom of Berkeley
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord Berkeley died in August 1810, Lord Berkeley died from plague during a small war where he lived, His whole entire family also received the plague that Lord Berkeley suffered from, but his body was to weak to survive from it. W
2 CONC hen William Berkeley applied to be summoned to the House of Lords as Earl of Berkeley. In March 1811 the Committee for Privileges decided that the Berkeley marriage of 1786 was "not then proved" and that the petitioner's claim was
2 CONC  not made out. William received Berkeley Castle and the other estates by will, and on 2 July, after the adverse decision of the Lords Committee, he claimed a writ of summons as a Baron as Baron by tenure of Berkeley Castle. The cl
2 CONC aim was fully laid before the Committee for Privileges in 1828 and 1829, but the Lords gave no judgement on the case. The eldest son born after the 1797 Lambeth marriage of the fifth Earl, Thomas Moreton FitzHardinge Berkeley, bec
2 CONC ame on the Earl's death in 1810 de jure 6th Earl of Berkeley — however, he refused to claim his right to the earldom. In 1831 William Berkeley was raised to the peerage as Baron Segrave, of Berkeley Castle in the County of Glouces
2 CONC ter.[3] Public appointments
2 CONT
2 CONT Berkeley was returned to parliament as one of two representatives for Gloucestershire in 1810 (succeeding his uncle Sir George Cranfield-Berkeley), a seat he only held until 1811.[4] In 1836 he was appointed Lord-Lieutenant of Glo
2 CONC ucestershire, a post he retained until his death.[1][5] In 1841 he was further honoured when he was made Earl FitzHardinge.[6] Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT Berkeley never married. He had several mistresses, and in 1821 John Waterhouse succeeded in an action for "criminal conversation" (adultery) against him, being awarded £1000 damages at Gloucester Assizes over Berkeley's affair wit
2 CONC h Mrs Waterhouse.[7] He died at Berkeley Castle, Gloucestershire, in October 1857, aged 70. The barony of Segrave and earldom of FitzHardinge died with him. The FitzHardinge title was revived in 1861 when his younger brother Mauri
2 CONC ce Berkeley was created Baron FitzHardinge.[1] References
2 CONT
2 CONT     thepeerage.com William FitzHardinge Berkeley, 1st and last Earl FitzHardinge
2 CONT     "The Peerage". Retrieved 26 December 2013.
