1 TITL Hon.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hon. Maria Margaret Craven was born on 26 April 1769. She was the daughter of William Craven, 6th Baron Craven of Hampsted Marshall and Lady Elizabeth Berkeley.1 She married William Philip Molyneux, 2nd Earl of Sef
2 CONC ton, son of Charles William Molyneux, 1st Earl of Sefton and Lady Isabella Stanhope, on 1 January 1792.1 She died on 9 March 1851 at age 81.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      Her married name became Molyneux.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Hon. Maria Margaret Craven and William Philip Molyneux, 2nd Earl of Sefton
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles William Molyneux, 3rd Earl of Sefton+
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Caroline Harriett Molyneux+ d. 8 Feb 1866
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Georgiana Frances Molyneux+ d. 26 Jun 1826
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Molyneux d. 3 May 1872
2 CONT
2 CONT Louisa Anne Maria Molyneux d. Jul 1855
2 CONT
2 CONT Frances Molyneux
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine Molyneux d. 25 Mar 1855
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt.-Col. George Berkeley Molyneux b. 16 Jul 1799, d. 27 Aug 1841
2 CONT
2 CONT Lt.-Col. Henry Richard Molyneux b. 27 Aug 1800, d. 23 May 1841
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Francis George Molyneux+ b. 5 Mar 1805, d. 24 May 1886
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 948. Hereinafter cited as Burke's Peera
2 CONC ge and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Born Maria Margaret Craven, she was the daughter of William Craven, 6th Baron Graven and Hampsted Marshall and Lady Elizabeth Berkley on April 26, 1769.  Her mother was the daughter of the 4th Earl of Berkley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On Jan. 1, 1792, Maria married William Philip Molyneux, Viscount Sefton, the son of Charles William Molyneux, 1st Earl of Sefton, and Isabella Stanhope (daughter of the 2nd Earl of Harrington).
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of Lady Sefton outside of her presence as a patroness of Almacks and being Lord Sefton;s wife. As a patroness her reputation is that of being good-natured. That she left blackballing to the other patronesses. The s
2 CONC ame with the revoking of vouchers of erring members. Lord Sefton was a very active member of society so it was natural that Maria become a patroness, especially as Lord Sefton’s mother was a founding member of Almacks. The couple 
2 CONC had at least five children, and possibly more. Maria was a patroness at least by 1812 and may at that time have been the oldest patroness of the group. The couple lived in Arlington Street and she would have been very busy managin
2 CONC g that household and her family. There were also residences at Stokes Farm in Berkshire and Croxteth Hall near Liverpool.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1814 Lord Sefton fell out with Prinny over trying to exclude the Prince Regent from the White’s Club Ball, which was cancelled. This may have caused Lady Sefton to be excluded from court functions as it did Lord Sefton. However
2 CONC  her activity as Patroness of Almacks did not decline for another ten years. William IV restored Lord Sefton to court life as William was an admirer of Sefton. Maria survived her husband by more than a decade dying January 1 of 18
2 CONC 51. https://thethingsthatcatchmyeye.wordpress.com/2014/10/19/regency-personalities-series-maria-molyneux-countess-of-sefton/
