1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1794 January 8th, born at Bayfordsbury, Hertfordshire,  the  7th son  of William  Baker, Esq.,  M.P.,  of  Bayfordbury'.
2 CONT
2 CONT George was the brother of Captain T.R. Baker.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Iberian Peninsular War 1808-1814''' (this was to play a major part in Napoleon's downfall)
2 CONT * 1809 15 year old George received his appointment as a cornet, carrying the troop standard.  [Cornet was originally the third and lowest grade of commissioned officer in a British cavalry troop, after captain and lieutenant. Abol
2 CONC ished in 1871, it is equivalent to today's Second Lieutenant]
2 CONT
2 CONT * 15 August 1811 - 16 year old George Baker served  as  an Ensign of  16th Lancers .  
2 CONT *  July 17 1812 - At Castrejon, as a junior Subaltern, he joined the 16th Light Dragoons (later the 16th/5th Lancers and now the Queen’s Royal Lancers.  He did not stay long with the Regiment, being captured three months later:
2 CONT * In the Battle of Venta del Pozo on 23 October 1812, an Anglo-German force led by Major-General Stapleton Cotton fought a rear-guard action against French cavalry under Major-Generals Jean-Baptiste Curto and Pierre Boyer.  At Tor
2 CONC quemada on October 24 1812 Lieutenant George Baker was taken prisoner  by the  French.  He was exchanged  2  years  later. 
2 CONT * Received Peninsula Medal with 2  Clasps
2 CONT
2 CONT [Source:  " The diary of a cavalry officer in the Peninsular and Waterloo Campaign, 1809 ...By William Tomkinson )] - 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1815, June 18 - fought  at Waterloo  as  an  Ensign, aged  21,  and was awarded  the  medal.
2 CONT
2 CONT (The Battle of Waterloo was fought on Sunday, 18 June 1815 near Waterloo in present-day Belgium, then part of The Netherlands.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===1822 Papers of Lieutenant George Baker are held at the Bodleian Library, Oxford (together with papers of  Dr. Clement John Baker) mainly relating to the period of Colonel Baker's office as British representative on the Greek Bo
2 CONC undary Commission.as follows:===
2 CONT
2 CONT 5em>* Baker | Caroline Julia | 1828-1836 (Person)
2 CONT 5em>* Baker | George | 1794-1859 | colonel (Person)
2 CONT 5em>* Greek Boundary Commission (Organisation)
2 CONT 5em>
2 CONT ===Married at Clare  Church,  Suffolk,  27  Feb.  1824===
2 CONT Caroline Julia,  dau.  of  Col.  John  Barker, Proprietor of Clare  Priory, Clare, Suffolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT They  had issue :-   
2 CONT * 3 daughters (one was Evelyn)  and 6 sons, of whom
2 CONT * the eldest son was in Regt 5-9th Pty,
2 CONT * one son was at Sandhurst,
2 CONT * George was at school and the 
2 CONT * next young sons were at school. (Source:  Emily Hills' handwritten family tree)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1826 Daughter Julia born.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1827 Papers of Lieutenant George Baker are held at the Bodleian Library, Oxford copies of a 'memorial' from Baker to the Duke of Wellington, 1827, and of a letter from him to Lord Palmerston in 1836 are part of the Baker Collectio
2 CONC n in the Bodleaian Library, Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===1830  he  was  appointed  H.B.M. Commissioner  for  determining  the  boundary  between Greece  and  Turkey.===
2 CONT   The Bodleaian Library Baker Collection has letters from the Foreign Office Ministers to George Baker:
2 CONT * Nov. 1830, the Earl of Aberdeen
2 CONT * Nov. 1830-1834, Lord Palmerston
2 CONT * 1835, the Duke of Wellington
