1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1739 Edward Conyers purchased the Elizabethan Copped Hall from Sir Thomas Webster who had owned it since 1701. Edward Conyers died three years later in 1742. '''Edward Conyers son, John,''' inherited the propert
2 CONC y and considered repairing it as it had become dilapidated.  His brother-in-law, Sir Roger Newdigate, carried out detailed drawings. However, plans for a new Georgian PalIadian mansion were drawn up by another architect.   In the 
2 CONC end only the main block was built - on a different site to the Elizabethan mansion - completed by 1758, with parts of the old house and gardens being retained.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1775 John Conyers died and his son, John, commissioned  architect James Wyatt to make internal changes.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The next member of the family to inherit Copped Hall was his son  '''Henry John Conyers (1782–1853)''' who was said to be so obsessed with hunting that he neglected the house badly. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Poll Record 2266, Copt Hall, Essex.  Freehold Houses and Land in Waxham
2 CONT * Name:	Henry John Conyers Esquire  
2 CONT * Poll Year:	1844
2 CONT * Parish or Rectory:	Waxham, County:	 Norfolk
2 CONT
2 CONT * Survived by three daughters, the house was finally sold by the family in 1869
