1 NOTE RULED FROM 1848-1916
1 NOTE (German Franz Josef) He was the last important ruler of the Habsburg 
2 CONT dynasty; his policies played a major role in the events that led to World 
2 CONT War I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Charles (his father) having renounced his right to the throne, 
2 CONT Francis Joseph became emperor when Ferdinand abdicated during the 
2 CONT revolution of 1848. With Russian help, he and his prime minister, Felix, 
2 CONT prince zu Schwarzenberg (1800-52), restored order in the empire and 
2 CONT reestablished Austrian dominance in the German confederation (1849-50).  
2 CONT Francis Joseph's failure to support Russia in the Crimean War (1854-56) 
2 CONT permanently damaged Austro-Russian relations, and in the decade that 
2 CONT followed, Austria lost most of its Italian possessions, as well as its 
2 CONT position of leadership in Germany. Weakened by these reverses, Francis 
2 CONT Joseph was forced to agree to Hungarian demands for autonomy in 1867, when 
2 CONT he and Elizabeth were formally crowned in Budapest as king and queen of 
2 CONT Hungary. He also planned to grant some form of self-government to the 
2 CONT Austrian Slavs but backed down because of opposition from the German and 
2 CONT Hungarian elite that controlled the new monarchy of Austria-Hungary. The 
2 CONT resulting dissatisfaction among Francis Joseph's Czechoslovakian and 
2 CONT Serbian subjects further weakened the Habsburg realms and caused increased 
2 CONT friction with Russia, which championed the cause of Europe's Slavic 
2 CONT peoples. Beginning in the 1870s, Austria-Hungary gradually became 
2 CONT subservient to its powerful neighbor and ally, the Prussian-dominated 
2 CONT German Empire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Joseph's later years were marked by a series of tragedies in his 
2 CONT family. In 1889 his only son and heir to the throne, Archduke Rudolf, 
2 CONT committed suicide; in 1898 his wife, the empress Elizabeth, was 
2 CONT assassinated by an Italian anarchist; and in 1914 his nephew, Francis 
2 CONT Ferdinand, who had replaced Rudolf as heir to the throne, was assassinated 
2 CONT by a Serbian nationalist. The murder of Francis Ferdinand precipitated the 
2 CONT crisis between Austria-Hungary and Germany on the one hand, and Serbia and 
2 CONT Russia on the other, that led to World War I. Francis Joseph did not live 
2 CONT to see Austria's defeat in the war and the extinction of the Habsburg 
2 CONT monarchy.
1 NOTE Francis Joseph I of the Hapsburg-Austria line, succeeded his uncle, Ferdinand I as Emperor of Austria in 1848.  Francis Joseph, also called Franz Josef, ruled until 1916. He was succeeded by his grand-nephew, Charles I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Joseph I of the Hapsburg dynasty came to rule Hungary during the reorganization of the dual Monarchy of Austria-Hungary in 1867. Its predecessor was the Austrian Empire founded in 1804 during the dissolution of the Holy Ro
2 CONC man Empire. Francis Joseph I was succeeded in 1916 by Charles I.
