1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_Gaetan,_Count_of_Girgenti '''en.wikipedia.org....'''] ; 
2 CONT * Prince Gaetan, Count of Girgenti,  Prince Gaetan, Count of Girgenti
2 CONT * Spouse 	Isabella, Princess of Asturias
2 CONT * Full name: Gaetano Maria Federico
2 CONT * House 	House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies
2 CONT * Father 	Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies
2 CONT * Mother 	Maria Theresa of Austria
2 CONT * Born 	12 January 1846, Naples, Two Sicilies
2 CONT * Died 	26 November 1871 (aged 25), Lucerne, Switzerland
2 CONT * Burial 	El Escorial, Madrid, Spain
2 CONT * Religion 	Roman Catholic
2 CONT * Prince Gaetano Maria Federico of Bourbon-Two Sicilies, Count of Girgenti[1][2] (Full Italian name: Gaetano Maria Federico, Principe di Borbone delle Due Sicilie, Conte di Girgenti[1][2]) (12 January 1846, Naples, Two Sicilies[1]
2 CONC [2] – 26 November 1871, Lucerne, Switzerland[1][2]) was the seventh child of Ferdinand II of the Two Sicilies and his wife Maria Theresa of Austria.[1][2] Gaetan was a member of the House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies and consort to Isa
2 CONC bella, Princess of Asturias, twice the recognized heir presumptive to the throne of Spain. Through this union, Gaetan was created an Infante of Spain.
2 CONT ===Marriage and later life===
2 CONT * Gaetan married Isabella, Princess of Asturias, eldest surviving child of Francis, Duke of Cádiz and his wife Isabella II of Spain, on 13 May 1868 in Madrid.[1][2] Gaetan was a first cousin to both of Isabella's parents. Their un
2 CONC ion was intended to end a feud between the Neapolitan Bourbons and the Spanish Bourbons following Spain's recognition of the Kingdom of Italy unified under the House of Savoy. The ceremony took place shortly before Spain's Gloriou
2 CONC s Revolution which brought an end to Isabella II's reign.
2 CONT * Gaetan and Isabella's marriage proved unhappy. For two years, Gaetan traveled throughout Europe visiting relatives in major cities including Vienna. A troubled and depressed man, Gaetan suffered from weak health and epilepsy. He
2 CONC  had unsuccessfully attempted suicide at least once before shooting himself in the head in his hotel room in Lucerne, Switzerland. Isabella returned to Spain in 1874 and did not remarry. Their brief union produced no issue.[1][2]
