0 @N6000000000337130585@ NOTE (Research):Emmanuel Philibert (Emanuele Filberto) Pronounced As: amanwel flber, 1528-80, duke of Savoy (1553-80), called Ironhead. He succeeded his father, Charles III, who had been dispossessed of his duchy by Francis I of France
1 CONC  and the Swiss in 1536. Emmanuel Philibert entered the service of Charles V, Holy Roman emperor and king of Spain, and later served Philip II of Spain. As Philip's lieutenant general in Flanders he won a brilliant victory over the
1 CONC  French at Saint-Quentin (1557) and captured the French commander, Anne de Montmorency. The Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis (1559) restored most of Savoy (except Vaud and Geneva, which remained Swiss) to Emmanuel Philibert, who in the 
1 CONC same year married Margaret of Valois, sister of Henry II of France. Savoy was in deplorable condition. The duke, with great energy and wisdom, reorganized its courts, finances, educational system, industry, and commerce. He also r
1 CONC eformed the army, substituting local militias for mercenaries. His skillful diplomacy rid Savoy of the French and Spanish garrisons and secured peaceful relations with the Swiss. Toward the Waldenses he displayed tolerance. By mak
1 CONC ing Turin his capital, he shifted the center of his duchy from Savoy proper to Piedmont, thus making it an Italian rather than a French state. He was succeeded by his son, Charles Emmanuel I.  Emmanuel Philibert Encyclop‘dia Brita
1 CONC nnica Article    born July 8, 1528, Chamb‚ry, Savoy died Aug. 30, 1580, Turin   Emmanuel Philibert, detail of a portrait by G. Vighi; in the Pinacoteca, Turin, Italy Alinari_Art Resource/EB Inc.   byname Emmanuel Philibert Iron-he
1 CONC ad,  French Emmanuelphilibert Tˆte De Fer,  Italian Emanuele Filiberto Testa Di Ferro duke of Savoy who recovered most of the lands his father Charles III had lost to France and Spain. A skilled soldier and a wily diplomat, he was
1 CONC  also an able administrator who restored economic equilibrium to Savoy while freeing it from foreign occupation.   Serving in the army of his mother's brother-in-law, the emperor Charles V, in the war against Francis I of France, 
1 CONC Emmanuel Philibert distinguished himself by capturing Hesdin (July 1553). When he succeeded his father a month later, he began the reacquisition of his lands. His brilliant victory over the French at Saint-Quentin (August 1557) on
1 CONC  the side of the Spanish solidified his power in Savoy. The Peace of Cateau-Cambr‚sis (1559), ending the wars between Charles V and the French kings, restored part of Emmanuel Philibert's duchy on the understanding that he marry M
1 CONC argaret of France, sister of King Henry II. Taking advantage of political struggles between the European powers, the duke slowly increased his domain, recovering possessions from the French, including Turin, and other possessions 
1 CONC from the Spanish, and buying two territories. He moved Savoy's capital from Chamb‚ry to Turin (1562), substituted Italian for Latin as the official language, and at the time of his death was arranging for the acquisition of the ma
1 CONC rquisate of Saluzzo.
0 @N6000000000337130525@ NOTE (Research):Charles Emmanuel I (Carlo Emanuele I)  1562-1630), duke of Savoy (1580-1630), son and successor of Emmanuel Philibert.  He continued his father's efforts to recover territories lost to the dutchy, but his reckless, alth
1 CONC ough cunning, diplomacy underminded many of the sound economic and political achievements of the previous decade.  His goal to incorporate Geneva, Saluzzo, and Montferrat into Savoy caused him to oscillate in his alliances between
1 CONC  France and Spain. In the long run he met only limited success. 1602 he tried unsuccessfully to reconquer Geneva by surprise attack.  He recovered Saluzzo from the French by the treaty of Lyons (1601), giving up in exchange, Bress
1 CONC e, Bugey, Gex, and Pinerolo, but he lost Saluzzo just before his death.  He waged war over the succession to Montferrat for much of the first quarter of the 16th century. At the time of his death his duchy was overrun by the Frenc
1 CONC h. Charles Emmanuel, called the Great, was succeeded by his son, Victor Amadeus I.  Charles Emmanuel I Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born Jan. 12, 1562, Rivoli, Savoy died July 26, 1630, Savigliano   Charles Emmanuel I, detai
1 CONC l of a lithograph  By courtesy of the Museo Centrale del Risorgimento, Rome   byname Charles Emmanuel The Great,  Italian Carlo Emanuele Il Grande duke of Savoy who alternated alliances with France and Spain, taking advantage of t
1 CONC he European power struggle in order to further his expansionist policy. A skilled soldier and shrewd politician, he was a capable ruler of Savoy, governing with moderation, promoting commercial development, and making his court a 
1 CONC centre of culture.   Although his pretensions to the throne of France were rejected, Charles Emmanuel benefitted from unrest in that country to seize the marquisate of Saluzzo (1588) and to invade Provence. The new French king Hen
1 CONC ry IV, however, waged a successful war against him, forcing him to cede three of his dynasty's possessions to the French, though he was allowed to retain Saluzzo. The defeated duke turned his attentions on Geneva but failed in his
1 CONC  surprise attack in December 1602.   By the Treaty of Bruzolo (April 1610), Charles Emmanuel aligned himself with the French against the Spanish and Austrian Habsburgs in exchange for a free hand in Lombardy. Although Henry IV's a
1 CONC ssassination aborted this alliance, Charles Emmanuel seized Monferrato from the Spanish in 1613, provoking a war that lasted until 1617, when he was forced to relinquish the duchy. In the Thirty Years' War, Charles Emmanuel, on th
1 CONC e promise of the imperial crown, allied himself with the enemies of the Habsburgs. Yet in December 1627, when he was promised Monferrato, he went over to the Spanish side. After suffering a serious defeat at the hands of the Frenc
1 CONC h in March 1629, he died, leaving his state prey to the warring armies.   Charles Emmanuel I Encyclop‘dia Britannica Article    born Jan. 12, 1562, Rivoli, Savoy died July 26, 1630, Savigliano   Charles Emmanuel I, detail of a lit
1 CONC hograph  By courtesy of the Museo Centrale del Risorgimento, Rome   byname Charles Emmanuel The Great,  Italian Carlo Emanuele Il Grande duke of Savoy who alternated alliances with France and Spain, taking advantage of the Europea
1 CONC n power struggle in order to further his expansionist policy. A skilled soldier and shrewd politician, he was a capable ruler of Savoy, governing with moderation, promoting commercial development, and making his court a centre of 
1 CONC culture.   Although his pretensions to the throne of France were rejected, Charles Emmanuel benefitted from unrest in that country to seize the marquisate of Saluzzo (1588) and to invade Provence. The new French king Henry IV, how
1 CONC ever, waged a successful war against him, forcing him to cede three of his dynasty's possessions to the French, though he was allowed to retain Saluzzo. The defeated duke turned his attentions on Geneva but failed in his surprise 
1 CONC attack in December 1602.   By the Treaty of Bruzolo (April 1610), Charles Emmanuel aligned himself with the French against the Spanish and Austrian Habsburgs in exchange for a free hand in Lombardy. Although Henry IV's assassinati
1 CONC on aborted this alliance, Charles Emmanuel seized Monferrato from the Spanish in 1613, provoking a war that lasted until 1617, when he was forced to relinquish the duchy. In the Thirty Years' War, Charles Emmanuel, on the promise 
1 CONC of the imperial crown, allied himself with the enemies of the Habsburgs. Yet in December 1627, when he was promised Monferrato, he went over to the Spanish side. After suffering a serious defeat at the hands of the French in March
1 CONC  1629, he died, leaving his state prey to the warring armies.
